-- allium: 3
-- score-contract.allium
--
-- The shared data contract of the PiX monorepo: what a score *is*.
--
-- @pix/editor and @pix/extension agree on this contract down to field
-- names, pixogram identifiers and library layout. This spec is the
-- canonical definition of that agreement — the Score / Movement /
-- ScoreStep shape, the cell encoding, stable identity, the split
-- between a partitura and its ScreenshotTrace, normalisation of
-- legacy data, and the local score library.
--
-- Consumers:
--   - packages/editor          authors and renders scores
--   - packages/extension       records scores from live browsing
--     (packages/extension/interaction-capture.allium)
--   - packages/editor/walkthrough-viewer.allium  replays a trace
--
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Scope
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Included:
--   - Score structure: a Score holds ordered Movements, each holding
--     ordered ScoreSteps. A step is one column of the grid; a layer is
--     one row. Layout (pix | sb) decides which layers are visible.
--   - Cell encoding: a cell is one string that optionally opens with a
--     pixogram reference. Both packages parse and emit this same form,
--     so it is contract, not presentation.
--   - Stable identity: score, movement and step ids, and the guarantee
--     that they survive a round trip out of the editor and back.
--   - Score state (draft | final) and who sets it.
--   - The partitura / trace split: a Score carries no capture-derived
--     data. Screenshots and focus regions live in a ScreenshotTrace
--     keyed by score, 1:1, append-only.
--   - Normalisation: how a score of unknown vintage is brought up to
--     the current contract on read (defaults, renames, id backfill,
--     shape coercion) and how embedded capture data is extracted into
--     a trace.
--   - The local score library: save, read, list, duplicate, delete,
--     capacity. Persistence is local to the browser profile.
--   - Serialisation forms for hand-off: an embed form (legacy, minimal,
--     size-stable) and a round-trip form (carries identity and state).
--   - The pixogram catalogue: a closed set of names, with per-layer
--     suggestions.
--
-- Excluded:
--   - Storage backend. The contract commits to "local to the browser
--     profile, persistent across sessions, never on the network".
--     IndexedDB, its store names and version numbers are implementation.
--   - Id generation scheme. The contract requires stable and unique;
--     the alphabet and length are implementation.
--   - Transport encoding of the serialisation forms (base64, URL
--     fragments). The contract fixes *what* each form carries, not
--     how it is packed for the wire.
--   - Pixogram artwork: the SVG files, their loading, caching and
--     preloading. The contract owns the catalogue of names, not the
--     drawings or their delivery.
--   - Everything the recorder does. How a step comes to exist from a
--     click is specified in interaction-capture.allium; this spec only
--     says what the resulting step must look like.
--   - Everything the editor does. Rendering, editing affordances,
--     export to PNG/SVG/PDF, i18n and routing are the editor's.
--   - The example scores bundled with the editor. They are content,
--     not contract.
--
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Ownership
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- This spec is the single owner of Score, Movement, ScoreStep,
-- ScreenshotTrace, RecordingSession, TraceSnapshot and TraceAnnotation.
-- interaction-capture.allium and walkthrough-viewer.allium reference
-- them; they do not define them.
--
-- Two questions that were open here are now settled, and both settle
-- the same way — towards what actually crosses the package boundary:
--
--   1. Cell shape. A cell is one encoded string, not a pair of icon
--      and text. A cell may carry a pixogram alone, text alone, or
--      both, and the encoding expresses all three without a special
--      case. This is the form that is stored, serialised and published
--      in embeds.
--
--   2. Layer naming. `supporting_processes` is the name of the layer,
--      everywhere. The short alias `supporting` is retired.

-- ============================================================
-- External Entities
-- ============================================================

-- Where scores live. Local to one browser profile, survives restart,
-- never leaves the device. Named so rules can speak about capacity and
-- persistence without naming a backend.
-- Capacity is bounded by weight, not by count. Twenty recordings full
-- of screenshots and twenty typed partituras are wildly different
-- loads on the profile's quota, and only one of them threatens it.
external entity ScoreLibrary {
    scores: List<Score> with library = this
    used_fraction: Decimal?                 -- 0..1 of the profile's quota, as
                                            -- reported by the browser; absent
                                            -- where the browser will not say

    -- Pressure is read from the quota where it is known. Where it is
    -- not, the count bound stands in as a coarse backstop, so a
    -- library on a browser that reports nothing still cannot grow
    -- without limit.
    is_under_pressure:
        if exists used_fraction:
            used_fraction >= config.library_pressure_fraction
        else:
            scores.count >= config.score_count_backstop
}

-- A score as it sits in storage, of unknown vintage. Fields are
-- optional because a score written by an older version may be missing
-- any of them, and the whole point of normalisation is to accept that.
-- Modelled as external because the contract does not own what past
-- versions wrote.
external entity StoredScore {
    id: String?
    title: String?
    description: String?
    layout: String?                         -- "pix" | "sb" | the retired "ip"
    state: String?                          -- "draft" | "final" | absent
    carries_inline_capture_data: Boolean    -- true for scores written before
                                            -- the partitura / trace split
}

-- A pixogram from the PiX vocabulary: monochrome icons identified by
-- name. The catalogue is closed — a name outside it is not a pixogram,
-- and a cell referencing one is read as plain text.
external entity Pixogram {
    name: String
    suggested_layers: Set<ScoreLayer>       -- narrows what the icon picker
                                            -- offers per layer; never restricts
                                            -- what a cell may reference
}

-- ============================================================
-- Enumerations
-- ============================================================

-- The five layers a score can have. Which are visible depends on
-- layout.
enum ScoreLayer {
    environment                             -- sb only
    | user
    | dialogue
    | system
    | supporting_processes                  -- sb only
}

enum ScoreLayout {
    pix                                     -- Interaction Process: 3 layers
    | sb                                    -- Service Blueprint: 5 layers
}

enum ScoreState {
    draft                                   -- recorded, awaits human refinement
    | final                                 -- authored, or a recording promoted by hand
}

-- What a serialised score carries when handed to another surface.
-- Neither form ever carries capture-derived data.
enum SerialisationForm {
    embed                                   -- legacy shape: content only, no identity.
                                            -- Consumed by the wiki.ead.pucv.cl embeds,
                                            -- whose size and shape must stay stable.
    | round_trip                            -- content plus identity and state, so
                                            -- re-opening updates the existing score
                                            -- instead of duplicating it
}

-- ============================================================
-- Value Types
-- ============================================================

-- One (layer x step) cell. Stored and exchanged as a single string
-- that may open with a pixogram reference followed by free text. The
-- pixogram and the text are derived from that string, not stored
-- beside it — see @invariant CellEncoding in the PiXScoreCodec
-- contract.
value Cell {
    raw: String                             -- "" for an empty cell

    icon: parse_pixogram_name(raw)          -- catalogue name, or null when the
                                            -- leading token is not one
    text: parse_cell_text(raw)              -- the remainder, trimmed
    is_empty: raw = ""
}

-- The highlighted region drawn over a screenshot. Percentages, not
-- pixels, so the highlight survives rescaling of the image.
value FocusRegion {
    x_percent: Decimal                      -- 0..100, centre x
    y_percent: Decimal                      -- 0..100, centre y
    radius_percent: Decimal                 -- 0..100, of half the longest edge
}

-- ============================================================
-- Entities
-- ============================================================

entity Score {
    id: String
    library: ScoreLibrary
    title: String
    description: String
    layout: ScoreLayout
    state: ScoreState
    movements: List<Movement> with score = this
    trace: ScreenshotTrace?                 -- absent for authored scores
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp

    visible_layers:
        if layout = pix:
            { user, dialogue, system }
        else:
            { environment, user, dialogue, system, supporting_processes }

    is_recording: exists trace
    is_editable: true                       -- every score is editable, recorded
                                            -- or not; only the trace is frozen

    transitions state {
        draft -> final                      -- MarkScoreAsFinal
        final -> draft                      -- ReopenScoreAsDraft
    }
}

-- An ordered run of steps. A score always has at least one movement;
-- a movement may be empty.
entity Movement {
    id: String
    score: Score
    position: Integer                       -- 0-based within score.movements
    steps: List<ScoreStep> with movement = this

    is_empty: steps.count = 0
}

-- One column of the grid: the partitura content of a single moment.
-- Carries no capture-derived data — that lives in the matching
-- TraceSnapshot, joined by step id.
--
-- The five cell fields are named for their layers, matching the
-- serialised field names exactly. A step carries all five regardless
-- of layout: layout decides what is rendered and serialised, not what
-- is stored, so switching sb -> pix -> sb is lossless.
entity ScoreStep {
    id: String
    movement: Movement
    position: Integer                       -- 0-based within movement.steps
    step_title: String                      -- non-empty marks a section start
    note: String                            -- caption below the grid

    environment: Cell
    user: Cell
    dialogue: Cell
    system: Cell
    supporting_processes: Cell

    starts_section: step_title != ""
    snapshot: TraceSnapshot?                -- the evidence behind this step, when
                                            -- the step was recorded rather than typed
}

-- The empirical record behind a recorded score. Authored scores have
-- none; a recorded score has exactly one, which grows across recording
-- sessions rather than being replaced by them.
entity ScreenshotTrace {
    id: String
    score: Score
    snapshots: List<TraceSnapshot> with trace = this
    sessions: List<RecordingSession> with trace = this
    created_at: Timestamp                   -- earliest captured_at among snapshots,
                                            -- or the moment of extraction when the
                                            -- snapshots carry no timestamp

    is_empty: snapshots.count = 0
    was_re_recorded: sessions.count > 1
}

-- One sitting at the recorder. Re-recording a score does not discard
-- what was captured before: it opens a new session, which appends a
-- new Movement to the partitura and new snapshots to the same trace.
--
-- The session is what makes the trace legible after a second pass.
-- Without it every snapshot would sit in one undifferentiated pile and
-- there would be nothing for a comparison view to compare — two runs
-- of the same task would be indistinguishable from one long run.
entity RecordingSession {
    id: String
    trace: ScreenshotTrace
    movement: Movement                      -- the movement this session appended
    index: Integer                          -- 0-based, in order of recording
    started_at: Timestamp
    ended_at: Timestamp?                    -- absent while the session is live

    is_live: not exists ended_at
    snapshots: TraceSnapshots where session = this
}

-- A free-form remark a person attaches to a captured moment, after the
-- fact: "aquí se confundió", "este paso sobra".
--
-- Separate from TraceSnapshot on purpose. The snapshot is evidence and
-- never changes; the annotation is commentary and changes freely.
-- Putting the remark on the snapshot would mean editing the record of
-- what was observed in order to say something about it.
entity TraceAnnotation {
    id: String
    snapshot: TraceSnapshot
    text: String
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp
}

-- One captured interaction. These fields are exactly the set that
-- historically lived inline on ScoreStep and were moved out; the
-- contract names them so extraction can be specified.
--
-- Interpreting these values is the recorder's and the viewer's
-- business, not the contract's — see interaction-capture.allium.
entity TraceSnapshot {
    id: String
    trace: ScreenshotTrace
    session: RecordingSession               -- which sitting produced it
    step: ScoreStep
    annotations: List<TraceAnnotation> with snapshot = this

    screenshot: String?                     -- the captured viewport
    focus: FocusRegion?
    captured_at: Timestamp?
    captured_from_url: String?
    captured_page_title: String?
    captured_kind: String?
    captured_tab_id: String?
    captured_trigger: String?
    boundary: String
}

-- ============================================================
-- Config
-- ============================================================

config {
    -- Fraction of the profile's storage quota at which the library is
    -- considered under pressure. The user is warned and offered a
    -- backup; nothing is evicted.
    library_pressure_fraction: Decimal = 0.8

    -- Coarse backstop for browsers that will not report a quota. Not
    -- the real bound — the real bound is weight — but a library that
    -- can measure nothing still must not grow without limit.
    score_count_backstop: Integer = 20

    -- Size of the pixogram catalogue. Named so a mismatch between the
    -- catalogue and the shipped artwork is a contract violation rather
    -- than a silently missing icon.
    pixogram_catalogue_size: Integer = 160

    -- Suffix appended to the title of a duplicated score.
    duplicate_title_suffix: String = " (copy)"

    -- Layout assumed when a score arrives without one.
    default_layout: ScoreLayout = pix

    -- State assumed for a score that predates the state field. Such
    -- scores are authored by definition — the extension has always
    -- written draft explicitly.
    legacy_default_state: ScoreState = final
}

-- ============================================================
-- Defaults
-- ============================================================

default Cell empty_cell = { raw: "" }

-- ============================================================
-- Rules — Reading and normalisation
-- ============================================================

-- Every read goes through normalisation. A caller never sees a score
-- of an older vintage, and never asks whether one needs migrating.
rule ReadScore {
    when: ReadScore(stored)

    ensures: ScoreRead(stored: stored)
}

rule ListScores {
    when: ListScores(library)

    ensures:
        for stored in stored_scores(library):
            ScoreRead(stored: stored)

    -- Ordering is a surface concern, guaranteed in CoreLibraryApi:
    -- most recently updated first.
}

-- Bring a stored score up to the current contract. In memory only —
-- the result reaches storage when the caller saves.
--
-- Total and idempotent: it accepts any stored score, including one
-- already current, and normalising twice changes nothing.
rule NormaliseScore {
    when: ScoreRead(stored)

    -- Field-level normalisation, in the order it matters:
    --   - layout: absent becomes config.default_layout; the retired
    --     name "ip" reads as pix; case is not significant.
    --   - title, description: absent become "".
    --   - shape: a bare list of steps reads as a single movement.
    --   - step fields: absent cells become empty_cell; the retired
    --     field name "enviroment" reads as environment; step_title
    --     and note absent become "".
    --   - state: anything other than draft or final becomes
    --     config.legacy_default_state.
    -- resolve_* are the black box functions that carry this out.
    ensures: Score.created(
        id: resolve_id(stored.id),
        title: resolve_text(stored.title),
        description: resolve_text(stored.description),
        layout: resolve_layout(stored.layout),
        state: resolve_state(stored.state),
        movements: resolve_movements(stored)
    )
}

-- Capture-derived data once lived inline on each step. Reading a score
-- of that vintage splits it: the step keeps its partitura content, the
-- capture fields become snapshots of a trace.
rule ExtractTrace {
    when: ScoreRead(stored)

    requires: stored.carries_inline_capture_data

    ensures: ScreenshotTrace.created(
        score: score_of(stored),
        snapshots: extract_snapshots(stored),
        created_at: earliest_capture_at(stored)
    )
    ensures: TraceExtracted(stored: stored)
}

-- Persisting the extracted trace is a repair, not part of the read's
-- contract. Split as its own rule so a failed repair cannot fail the
-- read: the next read simply retries.
rule PersistExtractedTrace {
    when: TraceExtracted(stored)

    ensures: SaveScore(score: score_of(stored))
}

-- ============================================================
-- Rules — Library
-- ============================================================

-- Saving never fails on capacity. Storage pressure is advisory: the
-- user is warned and offered a backup, and decides what to delete.
-- Refusing the save would strand work that has nowhere else to go —
-- the library is the only copy.
rule SaveScore {
    when: SaveScore(score)

    ensures: score.updated_at = now
    ensures: score.library.scores.add(score)
}

-- Fires when the library crosses into pressure, not on every save.
rule LibraryUnderPressure {
    when: library: ScoreLibrary.is_under_pressure

    ensures: StoragePressureReported(
        library: library,
        used_fraction: library.used_fraction
    )
}

rule CreateScore {
    when: CreateScore(library, layout, title?)

    ensures: Score.created(
        library: library,
        title: resolve_text(title),
        description: "",
        layout: layout,
        state: final,
        trace: null,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    -- A score created here is authored, so it starts final. Recordings
    -- are created by the extension and start draft; see
    -- interaction-capture.allium.
}

rule DeleteScore {
    when: DeleteScore(score)

    -- The cascade runs the whole depth: annotations hang off
    -- snapshots, snapshots and sessions off the trace, the trace off
    -- the score. Screenshots dominate the profile's quota, so anything
    -- left behind here is both orphaned and expensive.
    ensures: not exists score.trace
    ensures: not exists score
}

-- ------------------------------------------------------------
-- Annotations
-- ------------------------------------------------------------

rule AnnotateSnapshot {
    when: AnnotateSnapshot(snapshot, text)

    requires: text != ""

    ensures: TraceAnnotation.created(
        snapshot: snapshot,
        text: text,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )
}

rule EditAnnotation {
    when: EditAnnotation(annotation, text)

    requires: text != ""

    ensures: annotation.text = text
    ensures: annotation.updated_at = now
}

rule DeleteAnnotation {
    when: DeleteAnnotation(annotation)

    ensures: not exists annotation
}

rule DuplicateScore {
    when: DuplicateScore(source)

    ensures: Score.created(
        library: source.library,
        title: source.title + config.duplicate_title_suffix,
        description: source.description,
        layout: source.layout,
        state: source.state,
        movements: copy_movements_with_fresh_ids(source),
        trace: null,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )
}

-- ============================================================
-- Rules — State
-- ============================================================

rule MarkScoreAsFinal {
    when: MarkScoreAsFinal(score)

    requires: score.state = draft

    ensures: score.state = final
}

rule ReopenScoreAsDraft {
    when: ReopenScoreAsDraft(score)

    requires: score.state = final

    ensures: score.state = draft
}

-- ============================================================
-- Rules — Serialisation
-- ============================================================

rule SerialiseScore {
    when: SerialiseScore(score, form)

    -- A serialised step carries step_title, note and all five layer
    -- cells — not just the ones the current layout draws. See
    -- @invariant LayersAreHiddenNeverDiscarded: hiding a row must not
    -- be a way to lose its content. Beyond that, embed carries no
    -- identity and neither form carries capture data.
    ensures: SerialisedScore(
        score: score,
        form: form,
        payload: serialise(score, form)
    )
}

rule DeserialiseScore {
    when: DeserialiseScore(library, document)

    ensures: ScoreRead(stored: as_stored_score(document))

    -- A document carrying a known score id updates that score in
    -- place; one without enters the library as new. That follows from
    -- resolve_id preserving the id it is given, and SaveScore
    -- replacing by id.
}

-- ============================================================
-- Invariants
-- ============================================================

invariant StableIdsAreUnique {
    for a in Scores:
        for b in Scores:
            a != b implies a.id != b.id
}

invariant MovementIdsAreUnique {
    for a in Movements:
        for b in Movements:
            a != b implies a.id != b.id
}

invariant StepIdsAreUnique {
    for a in ScoreSteps:
        for b in ScoreSteps:
            a != b implies a.id != b.id
}

invariant TraceCardinality {
    -- At most one trace per score.
    for a in ScreenshotTraces:
        for b in ScreenshotTraces:
            a != b implies a.score != b.score
}

invariant SnapshotMatchesStep {
    -- Every snapshot points at a step of its own trace's score. The
    -- join is by step id, which is why step ids must survive
    -- serialisation.
    for snapshot in TraceSnapshots:
        snapshot.step.movement.score = snapshot.trace.score
}

invariant SnapshotAgreesWithStep {
    -- The two directions of the step/snapshot join agree.
    for step in ScoreSteps:
        exists step.snapshot implies step.snapshot.step = step
}

invariant StoragePressureIsAdvisory {
    -- Crossing config.library_pressure_fraction warns and offers a
    -- backup. It never refuses a save and never evicts a score.
    -- Eviction would destroy captured evidence that exists nowhere
    -- else, on a judgement the software is not in a position to make.
}

invariant SessionsAreOrdered {
    -- Session indices run 0..n-1 within their trace, in the order the
    -- sittings happened. The index is what a comparison view sorts by.
    for session in RecordingSessions:
        session.index >= 0
}

invariant OneLiveSessionPerTrace {
    -- A trace has at most one session still open. Two live sessions
    -- would interleave snapshots from different sittings into one
    -- movement, which is exactly what the session boundary exists to
    -- prevent.
    for a in RecordingSessions:
        for b in RecordingSessions:
            a != b and a.trace = b.trace implies not (a.is_live and b.is_live)
}

invariant SnapshotSessionMatchesTrace {
    for snapshot in TraceSnapshots:
        snapshot.session.trace = snapshot.trace
}

invariant SessionOwnsItsMovement {
    -- Each recording session appends exactly one movement, and that
    -- movement belongs to the trace's score.
    for session in RecordingSessions:
        session.movement.score = session.trace.score
    for a in RecordingSessions:
        for b in RecordingSessions:
            a != b implies a.movement != b.movement
}

invariant TraceBelongsToLibraryScore {
    -- A trace never outlives its score, so every trace resolves to a
    -- score still in a library. Deleting a score deletes its trace;
    -- without this an orphan trace occupies the profile's storage
    -- quota indefinitely, and screenshots dominate that quota.
    for trace in ScreenshotTraces:
        trace.score in trace.score.library.scores
}

invariant PositionsAreContiguous {
    -- Movement positions run 0..n-1 within their score, and step
    -- positions run 0..n-1 within their movement. No gaps, no
    -- duplicates. Deleting a step renumbers those after it.
}

invariant NoCaptureDataOnSteps {
    -- A ScoreStep never carries screenshot, focus, captured_* or
    -- boundary. That data belongs to TraceSnapshot. The split exists
    -- so a partitura can be edited, serialised and embedded without
    -- ever touching — or having to carry — the evidence behind it.
    --
    -- Legacy scores violate this by construction; ExtractTrace repairs
    -- them on read.
}

invariant TraceIsAppendOnly {
    -- Once written, a snapshot's capture-derived fields are never
    -- modified. A trace grows — during a session, and again when a
    -- later session appends to it — but nothing already in it is
    -- rewritten. Editing the partitura never edits the evidence, so
    -- what the score claims and what was observed can always be
    -- compared. See CaptureDerivedFieldsImmutable in
    -- interaction-capture.allium.
    --
    -- Two things sit deliberately outside this: TraceAnnotation, which
    -- is commentary rather than record, and which is why remarks live
    -- in their own entity; and the focus region, which is captured as
    -- observed and never re-cropped.
}

invariant FocusRegionIsNotEditable {
    -- The focus region marks what was actually targeted. It is
    -- sometimes tighter or looser than a person would draw by hand,
    -- and that is accepted: letting it be redrawn would make the
    -- evidence a matter of opinion, and the machinery to overlay a
    -- correction costs more than the imprecision does.
    --
    -- A person who wants to say the region is misleading says it in a
    -- TraceAnnotation.
}

invariant IdentityIsPreserved {
    -- An id already present is never replaced. Minting only fills
    -- gaps, on read and on duplication. Without this a score would
    -- lose its library identity on every read and duplicate itself on
    -- every save, and its trace would be orphaned because the join is
    -- by step id.
}

invariant LayersAreHiddenNeverDiscarded {
    -- Layout governs visibility alone. It never governs what is
    -- stored, and never governs what is serialised.
    --
    -- A score switched sb -> pix keeps the content of its environment
    -- and supporting_processes cells; the editor stops drawing those
    -- rows, and that is the whole of the effect. Switching back shows
    -- them again, unchanged. Serialising a score carries all five
    -- layers whatever the current layout, so a score that makes a
    -- round trip through an embed or a hand-off comes back whole.
    --
    -- The failure this forbids is silent and unrecoverable: a person
    -- flips to pix to tidy up the view, exports, re-imports, and two
    -- layers of work are gone with nothing to indicate it happened.
}

invariant PixogramCatalogueIsClosed {
    -- The catalogue is a fixed set of config.pixogram_catalogue_size
    -- names, shared by both packages. Per-layer suggestions narrow the
    -- set offered when picking an icon for a given layer; they never
    -- restrict what a cell may reference. Any pixogram is valid in any
    -- layer.
}

invariant LocalOnlyPersistence {
    -- The library lives in the browser profile. It is not synced, not
    -- backed up, and never sent anywhere. A score leaves the device
    -- only when the user serialises it deliberately.
}

-- ============================================================
-- Contracts
-- ============================================================

-- The encoding both packages must implement identically. This is the
-- one place where presentation and contract genuinely coincide,
-- because the encoded string is what crosses the package boundary.
contract PiXScoreCodec {
    parse_pixogram_name: (raw: String) -> String
    parse_cell_text: (raw: String) -> String
    serialise: (score: Score, form: SerialisationForm) -> String
    as_stored_score: (document: String) -> StoredScore

    @invariant CellEncoding
        -- A cell is one string. If its leading whitespace-delimited
        -- token is a pixogram reference — a catalogue name under a
        -- reserved prefix — the cell renders that pixogram followed by
        -- the rest of the string as text. Otherwise the whole string
        -- is text.
        --
        -- An unknown name is not an error and not a missing-icon
        -- placeholder: the cell reads as plain text, so a score
        -- authored against a newer catalogue degrades to something
        -- readable rather than something broken.

    @invariant NoScreenshotsOnTheWire
        -- Neither serialisation form carries screenshots. A single
        -- captured viewport is 100-300KB; a handful would exceed what
        -- a URL can carry. Traces move between surfaces by staying in
        -- the library, not by being serialised.

    @invariant EmbedShapeIsFrozen
        -- The embed form is consumed by live wiki.ead.pucv.cl embeds.
        -- Adding a field to it changes the size and shape of every
        -- published embed URL. New fields go in the round-trip form.
        --
        -- The five layer cells are not a new field: the embed form has
        -- always carried environment and supporting_processes whenever
        -- the step had them, without consulting the layout. Carrying
        -- them unconditionally is a clarification of existing
        -- behaviour, not a change to the frozen shape.

    @invariant StatePreservedOnRoundTrip
        -- A recording handed to the editor arrives still marked draft,
        -- so it lands among the recordings rather than among the
        -- finished scores. Dropping state here silently misfiles every
        -- recording.

    @invariant RoundTripIsLossless
        -- A score serialised in round_trip form and deserialised again
        -- is the same score: same identity, same content, same state,
        -- updating its library entry rather than duplicating it. Its
        -- trace stays where it was, still joined by step id, because
        -- step ids survived the trip.

    @guidance
        -- The two forms differ only in what identity they carry.
        -- Implement the embed form as the round-trip form minus
        -- identity and state, so the two cannot drift in how they
        -- render cells.
}

-- ============================================================
-- Surfaces
-- ============================================================

surface CoreLibraryApi {
    facing packages: PiXPackage

    contracts:
        fulfils PiXScoreCodec

    provides:
        ReadScore(stored: StoredScore)
        ListScores(library: ScoreLibrary)
        CreateScore(library: ScoreLibrary, layout: ScoreLayout, title: String?)
        SaveScore(score: Score)
        DeleteScore(score: Score)
        DuplicateScore(source: Score)
        MarkScoreAsFinal(score: Score)
        ReopenScoreAsDraft(score: Score)
        SerialiseScore(score: Score, form: SerialisationForm)
        DeserialiseScore(library: ScoreLibrary, document: String)
        AnnotateSnapshot(snapshot: TraceSnapshot, text: String)
        EditAnnotation(annotation: TraceAnnotation, text: String)
        DeleteAnnotation(annotation: TraceAnnotation)

    @guarantee MigrationIsInvisible
        -- A caller never asks whether a score needs normalising. Every
        -- read returns a score at the current contract. There is no
        -- migration step to run, no version to check, and no way to
        -- observe a half-migrated score.

    @guarantee ReadsDoNotFail
        -- A stored score that cannot be read yields nothing rather
        -- than an error that takes down the listing. One corrupt score
        -- must not make a library unopenable.

    @guarantee ListingIsMostRecentFirst
        -- ListScores returns scores ordered by updated_at, most
        -- recently touched first, so the library opens on what the
        -- user was last working on.

    @guarantee StoragePressureWarnsAndOffersBackup
        -- On crossing config.library_pressure_fraction the surface
        -- tells the user how full the library is and offers to export
        -- a backup before anything is deleted. It never refuses a save
        -- and never evicts. The choice of what goes is the user's,
        -- made with the evidence in front of them.
        --
        -- Where the browser reports no quota, the count backstop
        -- applies and the warning says so, rather than implying a
        -- measurement that was never taken.
}

-- ============================================================
-- Open Questions
-- ============================================================

-- All questions this spec opened have been answered by the author; the
-- decisions are recorded in the invariants and rules above. What
-- remains here are the consequences that landed in *code* rather than
-- in this document, and one question the answers created.

open question "Movement identity in the implementation: this spec gives Movement its own id, which is the agreed model. The implementation still carries `movement_ids` as a list running parallel to `score.scores[][]`, and migrate.js repairs a length mismatch by minting to fit — which can hand one movement another movement's id. Closing this is a data change, not a wording change: move the id onto the movement and migrate existing libraries. Until that lands, the spec and the storage disagree."

open question "Layer naming in the implementation: `supporting_processes` is now the single name in this spec. The editor still uses the short key `supporting` in LAYER_SUGGESTIONS and in the column list in PixScore.js, mapping between the two at the point of lookup. The alias should go."

open question "Comparison across sessions: re-recording appends a session and a movement, so comparing two runs means comparing two movements of one score. Steps are not aligned between them — the second run may take a different path, or the same path in a different number of steps. What does the viewer put side by side: step 1 against step 1 positionally, or some matching on captured_from_url and element kind? Positional is trivial and wrong the moment the paths diverge; matching is the useful thing and needs a rule for what counts as the same moment."
